export const DATA_72: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-105", num: 105, title: "Đề 105", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Banning the Bag: Countries Taking Action Against Plastic Pollution", paragraphs: ["Every year, the world produces approximately five trillion plastic bags. Most of these bags are used for only a matter of minutes before being discarded, yet they can persist in the natural environment for up to one thousand years without fully breaking down. In oceans, rivers, and landscapes across the planet, plastic bags have become one of the most visible symbols of the global plastic pollution crisis — choking marine animals, blocking drainage systems, and accumulating in vast floating patches of debris. In response to this growing environmental problem, governments in dozens of countries have introduced bans or restrictions on plastic bags over the past two decades, making it one of the most widely adopted environmental policy measures in recent history.", "Among the earliest and most frequently cited examples is Bangladesh, which became the first country in the world to ban thin plastic bags in 2002, after they were found to have blocked drainage systems and contributed significantly to the devastating floods that struck the country in 1988 and 1998. Kenya introduced one of the world's strictest plastic bag bans in 2017, imposing fines of up to forty thousand US dollars or prison sentences of up to four years for the manufacture, sale, or use of plastic bags — penalties so severe that they attracted international attention and considerable debate about proportionality. Rwanda, which implemented its ban in 2008, is now widely regarded as one of the cleanest countries in Africa, with its capital Kigali frequently praised by international visitors for its noticeably litter-free streets.", "In Europe, the approach has generally been less prohibitive and more graduated . The European Union introduced a directive in 2015 requiring member states to significantly reduce their consumption of lightweight plastic bags, but left individual countries to choose their own implementation methods. Some nations, including Ireland and Denmark, opted for plastic bag taxes rather than outright bans. Ireland's plastic bag tax, introduced as early as 2002, resulted in a ninety percent reduction in plastic bag use within just a few weeks of its introduction — a result that surprised even its designers and has since been studied as a model of effective environmental taxation. Denmark introduced a similar tax in the 1990s, making it one of the earliest adopters of this approach in Europe.", "The effectiveness of plastic bag bans and taxes varies considerably depending on how they are designed and enforced. Where bans apply only to very thin bags, retailers and consumers sometimes switch to slightly thicker bags that fall outside the legal definition, defeating the environmental purpose of the policy. In some countries, inadequate enforcement means that manufacturers continue to produce banned bags with minimal risk of penalty. Experts argue that the most successful policies are those that are broad in scope, accompanied by public awareness campaigns that explain the environmental reasons for the change, and supported by affordable and widely available alternatives such as reusable cloth bags or paper bags. Without these elements, even well-intentioned bans can fail to achieve meaningful reductions in plastic waste.", "Critics of plastic bag bans raise several concerns that deserve serious consideration. Some economists argue that bans are a blunt instrument that imposes costs on lower-income households, who rely on free plastic bags for practical purposes such as lining bins or carrying wet items, without providing them with affordable alternatives. Others point out that the production of reusable bags — particularly cotton tote bags — carries its own environmental cost: a cotton bag must be used many hundreds of times before its overall environmental impact is lower than that of the single-use plastic bags it replaces. These arguments do not necessarily undermine the case for action, but they do suggest that policy design matters enormously.", "Public attitudes toward plastic bag bans have shifted markedly over the past decade, driven by a growing awareness of plastic pollution fuelled by documentary films, social media campaigns, and scientific reports on the accumulation of microplastics in food chains and human bodies. In many countries, surveys now show that the majority of consumers support restrictions on single-use plastics, and that initial resistance to bag bans tends to diminish rapidly once people adapt to carrying reusable bags as a matter of habit. The momentum behind anti-plastic policies shows no sign of slowing, with increasing attention now turning to other single-use plastic items — straws, cutlery, packaging — as the next targets for regulation."], questions: [{ q: "What is the passage mainly about?", options: ["The scientific research into how long plastic bags take to decompose in different natural environments", "The global movement to ban or restrict plastic bags, including country examples, factors affecting success, and ongoing debates", "A comparison of ocean pollution levels in countries that have banned plastic bags and those that have not yet done so", "A detailed history of how plastic bags were invented and why they became so widely used across the world"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why did Bangladesh introduce its plastic bag ban in 2002?", options: ["An international environmental treaty signed in 2001 required all developing nations to eliminate single-use plastics by 2005.", "Plastic bags were found to have blocked drainage systems and contributed to catastrophic floods in 1988 and 1998.", "A major study revealed that plastic bags were the leading cause of air pollution in Dhaka and other major Bangladeshi cities.", "The government wanted to reduce the country's dependence on imported raw materials used in the manufacture of plastic bags."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"graduated\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["proceeding in stages or degrees rather than imposing an immediate and complete prohibition on the practice", "academically researched, based on peer-reviewed studies of the most effective approaches to environmental regulation", "formally approved, having received endorsement from all member states of the European Union before being introduced", "highly effective, producing measurable reductions in plastic bag use within a very short period of implementation"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what was the result of Ireland's plastic bag tax introduced in 2002?", options: ["It caused a ninety percent reduction in plastic bag use within just a few weeks, a result that surprised even its designers.", "It raised significant revenue for the government, which was used to fund a national recycling infrastructure programme.", "It led to widespread public protests from retailers who argued that the tax unfairly penalised small businesses.", "It reduced plastic bag use by approximately fifty percent over the first year, in line with the government's initial projections."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what penalties does Kenya impose for the manufacture, sale, or use of plastic bags?", options: ["Prison sentences of up to ten years for manufacturers and fines of up to one thousand dollars for individual consumers", "Fines of up to five thousand US dollars or community service orders of up to three months for first-time offenders", "Fines of up to forty thousand US dollars or prison sentences of up to four years for those found in violation of the ban", "Fines of up to ten thousand US dollars and mandatory participation in environmental education programmes"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"blunt\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["crude and indiscriminate, applying the same measure to all cases without accounting for differences in circumstances", "financially generous, providing benefits to a specific group of people without considering the wider social consequences", "technically complex, requiring significant administrative capacity to design, implement, and monitor effectively", "politically unpopular, likely to generate opposition from a large number of voters and consumer advocacy groups"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"these\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["the elements of broad scope, public awareness campaigns, and affordable alternatives that make plastic bag bans effective", "the manufacturers who continue to produce banned bags despite the existence of regulations prohibiting their sale", "the retailers and consumers who switch to slightly thicker bags that fall outside the legal definition of a ban", "the environmental reasons for plastic bag restrictions that governments must communicate clearly to the public"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what concern do critics raise about cotton tote bags as alternatives to plastic bags?", options: ["Cotton tote bags are less durable than plastic bags and need to be replaced frequently, generating significant textile waste.", "Cotton tote bags are not widely available in developing countries, limiting their usefulness as a practical alternative to plastic.", "Cotton tote bags must be used hundreds of times before their overall environmental impact is lower than single-use plastic bags.", "Cotton tote bags are more expensive to produce than plastic bags, making them unaffordable for lower-income households."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as an element that makes plastic bag bans more successful?", options: ["The provision of affordable and widely available alternatives such as reusable cloth bags or paper bags", "The availability of financial penalties for producers who continue to manufacture banned bags after the policy takes effect", "A broad scope of application that covers a wide range of bag types rather than only the thinnest varieties", "Public awareness campaigns that explain the environmental reasons behind the restrictions to ordinary consumers"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What does the author suggest about public attitudes toward plastic bag bans in the final paragraph?", options: ["Public attitudes vary widely between developed and developing countries, making global policy coordination very difficult.", "Public support for bag bans has grown significantly, and initial resistance typically fades once people form new habits.", "Most people remain strongly opposed to plastic bag bans because they find reusable bags inconvenient and expensive to purchase.", "Younger generations are enthusiastic about plastic bag bans, while older consumers continue to resist the change."], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "From Renting DVDs to Making Oscars: Netflix and the Original Content Revolution", paragraphs: ["When Netflix began its journey in 1997, its business model was straightforward and its ambitions appeared modest: the company offered American customers the ability to rent DVD films by mail, selecting titles from an online catalogue and receiving them at home within a few days. Within a decade, the company had pivoted to streaming, making its library of licensed content available over the internet to subscribers who paid a flat monthly fee. By 2013, however, Netflix had embarked on a strategic transformation that would fundamentally alter not only its own business but the broader landscape of global entertainment: it began producing its own original content. The release of House of Cards in February of that year — delivered to subscribers all at once in a full-season drop that invented the practice now known as binge-watching — signalled that Netflix was no longer merely a distributor of other companies' creative work. It intended to be a studio.", "The strategic logic behind Netflix's move into original production was driven by several converging pressures. As the company's streaming service grew in scale, the licensing fees demanded by major Hollywood studios and television networks for their back catalogues and current programming escalated correspondingly. Studios that had initially been willing to licence their content to Netflix at relatively modest rates began to recognise that they were effectively helping to build a competitor, and several — including Disney, NBC Universal, and WarnerMedia — eventually withdrew their content to launch their own streaming services. This progressive erosion of its licensed library left Netflix with an urgent need to generate compelling content that it owned outright — content that could not be withdrawn by a rival studio and that would, over time, become the primary reason subscribers chose to pay for the service rather than switching to a competitor.", "The financial commitment required to execute this strategy has been staggering. Netflix's annual content spending, which stood at approximately three billion dollars in 2013, had grown to more than seventeen billion dollars by 2021, a figure that exceeded the total content budgets of several major traditional broadcasters. This investment has been deliberately global in scope: rather than producing content exclusively for its largest English-speaking markets, Netflix has funded original productions in dozens of languages and from dozens of countries, including Spanish-language thrillers from Spain and Colombia, Korean dramas, German science fiction series, and Nigerian feature films. The global reach of its distribution platform gives Netflix a structural advantage over traditional national broadcasters, since a production that appeals strongly to audiences in one country can generate value across the company's entire subscriber base worldwide.", "The creative and commercial outcomes of this strategy have been uneven but cumulatively impressive. Netflix has produced a number of productions that have achieved genuine cultural impact and critical recognition — among them Stranger Things , The Crown , Squid Game , and Roma , the latter becoming the first streaming-service production to win the Academy Award for Best Picture in all but name, taking the top prize at several major awards ceremonies. At the same time, the sheer volume of content Netflix commissions has inevitably produced a large number of productions that attract little attention and disappear quickly from the platform's catalogue. Critics argue that the company's data-driven commissioning process — in which content decisions are heavily influenced by subscriber behaviour analytics rather than purely by editorial judgment — tends to favour familiar genres and accessible formats over genuinely challenging or experimental work.", "Netflix's shift to original production has also altered the economics and working practices of the broader entertainment industry in ways that are still being fully understood. The company's willingness to pay above-market rates for high-profile talent — writers, directors, and actors — has driven up production costs across the industry, making it more difficult for smaller studios and independent producers to compete for the same creative resources. At the same time, Netflix's global distribution model has provided an unprecedented platform for creators from markets that were previously marginalised in international entertainment — the extraordinary global success of the Korean series Squid Game in 2021, which became the most-watched programme in Netflix history at the time of its release, offered a striking illustration of how the company's infrastructure can amplify content that might once have remained largely invisible to non-Korean audiences.", "The competitive landscape that Netflix helped to create has now turned against it to some degree. The proliferation of rival streaming services — Disney+, HBO Max, Apple TV+, Amazon Prime Video, and numerous others — has fragmented the audience and intensified the battle for subscriber attention in ways that place continuous upward pressure on content spending. Netflix's subscriber growth, which had seemed essentially limitless during the pandemic, slowed sharply in 2022, prompting the company to introduce an advertising-supported tier and to crack down on the widespread practice of password sharing. Whether the company that invented the streaming era can sustain its position at the top of an industry it created remains one of the most closely watched questions in global media.", "What is not in doubt is that Netflix's transition from a DVD rental service to a global content studio has permanently reshaped how entertainment is produced, distributed, and consumed — and that the full consequences of that transformation, for creators, audiences, and the cultural life of nations around the world, are still unfolding."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of this passage?", options: ["Netflix transformed from a DVD rental service into a global content studio, a shift that has reshaped the entertainment industry but now faces new competitive pressures.", "Streaming services like Netflix have made traditional cinema and broadcast television largely irrelevant to younger generations of viewers worldwide.", "Netflix's decision to produce original content was a financial miscalculation that has left the company vulnerable to competition from larger studios.", "The success of international productions such as Squid Game proves that non-English-language content is more popular with global audiences than Hollywood films."], answer: 0 }, { q: "Why does the author mention the release of House of Cards in paragraph 1?", options: ["To demonstrate that political drama was the genre that Netflix identified as most likely to attract high-value subscribers in its early years", "To illustrate that Netflix's decision to deliver an entire season at once created a new viewing behaviour and signalled its ambitions as a studio", "To explain why subscribers began cancelling their Netflix memberships in favour of services offering more traditional weekly episode releases", "To suggest that House of Cards was the most critically acclaimed television production in the history of the streaming industry"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, approximately how much did Netflix spend on content annually by 2021?", options: ["More than seventeen billion dollars, a figure that exceeded the total content budgets of several major traditional broadcasters", "Over thirty billion dollars, making Netflix the single largest investor in entertainment content anywhere in the world", "Approximately ten billion dollars, representing a significant increase but still below the budgets of major traditional broadcasters", "Around three billion dollars, the same level as its initial content budget when it first began producing original programming in 2013"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"uneven\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["variable in quality and impact, with some productions achieving great success and others attracting little attention", "deliberately experimental, prioritising artistic risk-taking and innovation over commercial appeal and subscriber growth", "consistently outstanding, producing a steady stream of critically acclaimed productions that have all attracted large global audiences", "commercially disappointing, failing to generate sufficient subscriber interest to justify the scale of investment involved"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what was significant about the global performance of the Korean series Squid Game in 2021?", options: ["It prompted Netflix to increase its investment in Korean drama productions by more than two hundred percent in the following year.", "It was the first Korean production to be nominated for an Academy Award, marking a breakthrough for Asian cinema in Hollywood.", "It became the most-watched programme in Netflix history at the time, demonstrating how the platform can amplify previously marginalised content globally.", "It attracted more new subscribers to Netflix in a single month than any previous original production in the company's history."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"proliferation\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["technological improvement, as streaming services upgraded their platforms to offer higher video quality and more personalised recommendations", "rapid increase in number, resulting in a crowded and highly competitive market with many services competing for the same audience", "sudden disappearance, caused by the financial difficulties that many streaming services experienced following the end of the pandemic period", "gradual consolidation, as smaller streaming platforms merged with larger ones to achieve the scale needed to compete with Netflix"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"This\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["the structural advantage that Netflix holds over national broadcasters because of its global distribution platform", "the progressive withdrawal of licensed content by rival studios seeking to launch their own competing streaming services", "the decision to fund original productions in dozens of languages and from dozens of different countries simultaneously", "Netflix's annual content spending, which had grown to more than seventeen billion dollars by 2021"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following actions did Netflix take in response to slowing subscriber growth in 2022?", options: ["It launched an advertising-supported subscription tier and began taking action against widespread password sharing among users.", "It withdrew from several international markets to concentrate its resources on defending its position in the United States.", "It significantly reduced its original content spending and focused instead on acquiring licences for popular existing programmes.", "It merged with a major Hollywood studio to gain access to a larger library of licensed content at a lower cost."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a reason that drove Netflix toward producing its own original content?", options: ["The need to own content outright so that it could not be withdrawn by a competing studio at any point in the future", "Pressure from investors who demanded that Netflix demonstrate its ability to win major international awards for its productions", "Rising licensing fees demanded by major Hollywood studios as Netflix's streaming service grew in scale and value", "The withdrawal of content by rival studios that launched their own streaming services, eroding Netflix's licensed library"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Based on paragraphs 4 and 6, what can be inferred about Netflix's current position in the streaming industry?", options: ["Netflix has effectively eliminated competition from rival streaming services and now enjoys a more dominant market position than at any previous point in its history.", "Netflix is likely to abandon original content production in the near future and return to a business model based primarily on licensing content from other studios.", "Netflix's subscriber base has continued to grow rapidly despite increased competition, confirming that its strategy of investing heavily in original content has been fully vindicated.", "Netflix faces growing pressure from both the competitive landscape it helped create and internal questions about whether its commissioning strategy produces sufficiently distinctive content."], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "Between the Clinic and the Herbalist: Shifting Attitudes Toward Traditional and Modern Medicine", paragraphs: ["For most of human history, the boundary between what we now call traditional medicine and what we call modern, or biomedicine, did not exist — because biomedicine itself did not exist. The elaborate systems of herbal therapy, dietary regulation, manual manipulation, and spiritual healing that developed independently across ancient China, India, Greece, the Arab world, and indigenous cultures on every inhabited continent were simply medicine: the accumulated practical wisdom of generations responding to sickness and injury with the tools available to them. [1] It was only with the emergence of experimental physiology, germ theory, and evidence-based clinical practice in the nineteenth and twentieth centuries that a new form of medicine came to define itself partly in opposition to what had come before, casting traditional approaches as pre-scientific remnants to be superseded rather than bodies of knowledge to be studied.", "This dismissive attitude reached its apex in the mid-twentieth century, a period of extraordinary confidence in the capacity of pharmaceutical science and surgical technique to eliminate or manage virtually any disease. The development of antibiotics, vaccines, and advanced surgical procedures produced genuine and dramatic improvements in human health outcomes that seemed to vindicate the biomedical approach comprehensively. Traditional healers, herbalists, and practitioners of systems such as Traditional Chinese Medicine and Ayurveda were frequently marginalised within formal healthcare structures, and in some contexts their practices were actively suppressed by regulatory authorities who regarded them as unproven at best and dangerous at worst. Patient demand for traditional therapies, however, never disappeared entirely — even in societies where biomedicine was most thoroughly institutionalised.", "The reassessment of traditional medicine began in earnest in the latter decades of the twentieth century, driven by several converging forces. A growing body of patients in high-income countries reported dissatisfaction with the limitations of conventional medicine — its tendency to treat symptoms rather than underlying causes, its perceived lack of attention to the patient as a whole person, and the side effects of many pharmaceutical treatments. Simultaneously, ethnobotanical researchers began documenting that traditional plant-based knowledge contained pharmacologically significant compounds that had been overlooked by mainstream science. [2] The isolation of artemisinin from a plant used in Chinese medicine, which subsequently became the world's most effective antimalarial treatment, became the most celebrated illustration of this productive intersection between traditional knowledge and scientific investigation.", "By the 1990s and 2000s, the term \"integrative medicine\" had entered clinical and policy discourse in many countries, signalling an aspiration to combine the strengths of both traditions rather than forcing a choice between them. Hospitals in the United States, Germany, and Australia began establishing integrative medicine departments that offered acupuncture, mindfulness-based stress reduction, and herbal consultation alongside conventional treatments. The World Health Organization, which had previously paid relatively little attention to traditional medicine, published its first global strategy on the subject in 2002 and updated it in 2014 and 2019, formally recognising traditional medicine as a significant component of primary healthcare in many parts of the world and encouraging member states to develop regulatory frameworks that could ensure its safety while preserving its accessibility. [3]", "The shift in attitudes has not, however, been uniform or uncritical. The integration of traditional therapies into formal healthcare settings has generated substantial methodological debate about how such therapies should be evaluated. Randomised controlled trials — the gold standard of biomedical evidence — are poorly suited to evaluating holistic treatments whose effects may depend on the therapeutic relationship, the individuality of the prescription, or complex interactions among multiple active compounds. Critics of integrative medicine argue that endorsing therapies without rigorous proof of efficacy risks exposing patients to treatments that are ineffective or, in some cases, actively harmful. Proponents respond that the evidentiary standards appropriate for a pharmaceutical drug are not necessarily appropriate for a traditional practice that has been refined through centuries of clinical observation. This tension between different epistemologies — different theories of how knowledge is acquired and validated — remains one of the most productively unresolved questions in contemporary healthcare.", "The geography of this debate also matters. In many low- and middle-income countries, traditional medicine is not a lifestyle choice made by consumers seeking alternatives to a functional healthcare system — it is the primary or only form of healthcare available to large segments of the population. For these communities, the question is not whether to integrate traditional and modern medicine but how to ensure that both are practised as safely and effectively as possible. In China, a formal system of integrated Chinese and Western medicine has operated within the national health system for decades, providing a model — imperfect and politically complex, but functional — of how the two traditions can coexist within a single institutional framework. [4]", "What the evolution of attitudes toward traditional medicine ultimately reflects is a broader epistemological maturation within biomedicine itself: a growing recognition that the confidence of mid-twentieth-century medical science was in some respects premature, that different forms of knowledge can contribute meaningfully to human health, and that the goal of healthcare is not the triumph of one tradition over another but the wellbeing of patients. This recognition does not require abandoning the principles of evidence and critical inquiry — it requires applying them with greater sophistication, humility, and cultural awareness than has always been the case in the past."], questions: [{ q: "What is the central argument developed throughout this passage?", options: ["Attitudes toward traditional medicine have evolved from dismissal to cautious reappraisal, driven by patient dissatisfaction, scientific discovery, and a growing recognition that both traditions have value.", "The WHO's global strategy on traditional medicine has single-handedly transformed how governments and medical institutions across the world treat alternative health practices.", "Traditional medicine has been conclusively proven inferior to biomedicine in all clinical contexts and should be retained only as a cultural practice rather than a health intervention.", "The integration of traditional and modern medicine is a policy that has been successfully implemented worldwide and represents the future of all national healthcare systems."], answer: 0 }, { q: "What is the author's purpose in describing the development of antibiotics and vaccines in paragraph 2?", options: ["To explain why mid-twentieth-century medical confidence was so high, and why this confidence contributed to the marginalisation of traditional practices.", "To demonstrate that pharmaceutical science has now reached its limits and can no longer produce the kinds of breakthroughs that occurred in the twentieth century.", "To argue that biomedical achievements have been overstated and that traditional medicine was more effective than historians have acknowledged.", "To suggest that traditional medicine should have been integrated into the development of antibiotics and vaccines from the very beginning."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why is artemisinin considered a significant example in the context of this passage?", options: ["It illustrated how traditional plant-based knowledge can lead to the discovery of pharmacologically important compounds recognised by mainstream science.", "It showed that ethnobotanical research produces better antimalarial treatments than laboratory-based pharmaceutical development.", "It proved that Ayurvedic medicine is scientifically superior to Traditional Chinese Medicine in the treatment of infectious diseases.", "It was the first compound ever derived from a plant, demonstrating that botanical research can produce commercially viable pharmaceutical treatments."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, when did the World Health Organization first publish a global strategy on traditional medicine?", options: ["In 2019, prompted by growing international pressure from patient advocacy groups and traditional medicine practitioners", "In 2002, with subsequent updates issued in 2014 and 2019 recognising traditional medicine as a significant healthcare component", "In 2014, following a decade-long review of evidence on the safety and effectiveness of traditional medical practices worldwide", "In 1990, as part of a broader initiative to reform primary healthcare provision in developing nations"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the example of China illustrate in the context of integrating traditional and modern medicine?", options: ["That low-income countries are better positioned than high-income countries to integrate traditional and modern medicine due to lower costs.", "That traditional Chinese medicine is now regarded as fully equivalent to Western biomedicine in all clinical settings across the country.", "That government-mandated integration of traditional and modern medicine inevitably creates political conflict that undermines the quality of both systems.", "That a formal system of integrated Chinese and Western medicine operating within a national health framework is practically achievable, despite its complexity."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"epistemologies\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["the financial models and resource allocation strategies that govern how healthcare is funded and delivered in different countries", "the theoretical frameworks concerning how knowledge is produced, validated, and justified within different intellectual traditions", "the professional qualifications and licensing requirements that practitioners must meet before they are permitted to treat patients", "the clinical protocols and administrative procedures that different healthcare systems use to regulate the practice of medicine"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"vindicate\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["to formally adopt a practice as the official policy of a regulatory or governmental authority after a period of review", "to prove that something was correct or justified, clearing it of doubt or criticism through compelling evidence or results", "to partially support a position while acknowledging that significant weaknesses and counterarguments still remain unresolved", "to popularise a particular approach among the general public through widespread media coverage and government endorsement"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["the pharmaceutical treatments and surgical procedures that produced dramatic improvements in health outcomes in the mid-twentieth century", "the patients who continued to seek traditional therapies even in societies where biomedicine was most thoroughly institutionalised", "the traditional healers, herbalists, and practitioners of systems such as Traditional Chinese Medicine and Ayurveda", "the regulatory authorities who were responsible for evaluating and approving both conventional and traditional medical practices"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a reason why patients in high-income countries grew dissatisfied with conventional medicine?", options: ["The side effects associated with many pharmaceutical treatments prescribed by conventional practitioners", "A perceived lack of attention to the patient as a complete person rather than a set of symptoms", "The high financial cost of pharmaceutical treatments relative to traditional herbal therapies", "A tendency to address symptoms rather than the underlying causes of illness"], answer: 2 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This critique gained particular force as patients began turning to traditional practitioners for conditions that conventional medicine had failed to resolve satisfactorily.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "The Weight of Welcome: Japan's Rice Bag Tradition and the Anthropology of Birth Ritual", paragraphs: ["In Japan, the arrival of a newborn child has long been accompanied by a set of rituals whose symbolism encodes deeply held cultural beliefs about health, community, and the relationship between the living and the sacred. Among the most distinctive of these practices — and one that has drawn increasing scholarly attention as Japan's demographic anxieties have intensified — is the custom of presenting a bag of rice to the parents of a newborn, sized and weighted to correspond precisely to the birth weight of the child. [1] The practice, known colloquially as \"taijuu-betsu omusubi\" or, in its more commercially packaged form, as the \"birth weight rice gift,\" has roots that intertwine agricultural tradition, Shinto spiritual belief, and the intensely relational social logic of Japanese gift exchange — though its contemporary expression has been substantially shaped by the packaging industry and the gift economy of modern urban Japan.", "To appreciate the cultural depth of this tradition, it is necessary to understand the central place of rice in Japanese civilisation. Rice has occupied a position in Japanese culture that goes considerably beyond its role as a dietary staple. In Shinto belief, rice is associated with Inari, the deity of agriculture and prosperity, and the cultivation and sharing of rice has historically been bound up with concepts of communal obligation, seasonal rhythm, and divine favour. The offering of rice at births, weddings, funerals, and harvest festivals reflects a theology in which the material and the sacred are not sharply distinguished — in which a grain is also a benediction . The birth weight rice gift participates in this symbolic tradition while also performing a more immediate social function: it gives physical, tangible form to a number which, in the context of a newborn's arrival, carries enormous emotional significance for parents and grandparents alike.", "The practice of calibrating a gift to the birth weight of a child reflects a broader logic of mimetic representation that recurs across Japanese material culture. By lifting a bag of rice that weighs exactly as much as the newborn, a grandparent, aunt, or close family friend performs a kind of embodied understanding — they experience, through their own muscles and hands, something of the physical reality of the child they may not yet have had the opportunity to hold. For grandparents who live at a distance, or elderly relatives for whom travelling to meet the infant is difficult, this tactile dimension of the gift carries particular emotional weight. The bag does not merely represent the child symbolically; it instantiates the child's presence in a form that can be passed between hands, lifted, and held. [2]", "The commercial packaging of the birth weight rice gift has evolved considerably since the practice began to attract wider attention in the 1990s. Today, specialist producers offer bags printed with the child's name, date of birth, and birth weight, often accompanied by a certificate of authenticity confirming the weight of the rice enclosed. Some products include photographs of the infant printed on the packaging, while others are designed as decorative objects intended to be displayed rather than consumed. Premium versions may incorporate rice varieties — such as the prized Koshihikari strain from Niigata prefecture — that carry their own cultural cachet, adding a further layer of meaning to the gesture. The gift has also migrated online, with several producers operating nationwide shipping services that allow family members living far from the new parents to send a bag within days of receiving news of the birth. [3]", "Anthropologists who have studied the practice situate it within Japan's broader culture of gift exchange, known as \"omiyage,\" in which the giving and receiving of gifts performs important social functions beyond the mere transfer of material goods. Japanese gift culture is characterised by a high degree of attention to packaging, presentation, and symbolic appropriateness — the gift must communicate not only goodwill but also a precise calibration of the social relationship between giver and recipient. The birth weight rice gift is unusually well suited to this cultural logic because its central conceit — the correspondence between the weight of the rice and the weight of the child — is simultaneously universal in its emotional appeal and specific enough to feel deeply personal. Unlike a generic baby gift, it cannot be repurposed for any other occasion; it is irreversibly tied to a particular child, a particular weight, and a particular moment in time.", "The practice has also attracted attention from demographers and cultural commentators in the context of Japan's well-documented fertility crisis. Japan's total fertility rate has fallen well below the replacement level of 2.1 children per woman, and the government has introduced a range of financial incentives and parental leave policies in an attempt to encourage family formation. Some observers have suggested that cultural practices that celebrate and mark the birth of a child — investing the event with symbolic weight, community recognition, and ritual significance — may play a role, however modest, in shaping the social environment within which decisions about parenthood are made. The birth weight rice tradition, in this reading, is not merely a charming custom but a form of social infrastructure: a way of telling new parents that their child's arrival matters, that it has been noticed, measured, and welcomed by the community that surrounds them. [4]", "Whether such an interpretation overstates the sociological significance of a gift-giving practice is a question that researchers are unlikely to resolve with confidence. What is clearer is that the birth weight rice tradition illuminates, with unusual vividness, the way in which apparently simple material objects can serve as vehicles for cultural meaning, social relationship, and collective values — and that the study of such practices offers insights into a society's deepest assumptions about what a new life is worth and what it is owed."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following most accurately represents the central claim of this passage?", options: ["The birth weight rice gift is a culturally layered practice whose symbolic, social, and demographic dimensions reveal deep assumptions about community, belonging, and the value of new life.", "Japan's omiyage gift culture is uniquely suited to modern commercial packaging, making it more resilient than traditional gift-giving practices in other East Asian societies.", "The decline of traditional birth rituals such as the rice bag gift is a direct consequence of Japan's falling birth rate and the progressive erosion of Shinto religious practice.", "Japan's birth weight rice tradition is primarily a commercial phenomenon whose cultural significance has been greatly exaggerated by anthropologists and cultural commentators."], answer: 0 }, { q: "What is the author's attitude toward the claim that birth rituals like the rice bag tradition may influence decisions about parenthood in Japan?", options: ["Strongly supportive, arguing that cultural rituals are the most powerful factor shaping fertility decisions in any society", "Openly critical, warning that overstating the sociological significance of gift-giving practices misleads policymakers about the causes of low fertility", "Cautiously agnostic, acknowledging the interpretation while noting that researchers are unlikely to resolve its validity with confidence", "Dismissive, suggesting that cultural practices have no meaningful effect on demographic trends and should not be studied in this context"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"mimetic\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["involving imitation or representation, recreating the qualities of something else through a corresponding physical form", "involving the exchange of symbolic objects between members of a community to reinforce shared cultural values", "involving the commercial reproduction of traditional practices in forms that are accessible to a wider contemporary audience", "involving the physical measurement and precise calibration of an object to ensure it meets a specified standard of accuracy"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what role does rice play in Shinto belief?", options: ["Rice is considered a purely secular staple food in Shinto tradition, with no spiritual associations in formal religious ceremonies.", "Rice is regarded as a sacred object that may only be handled by ordained Shinto priests during officially sanctioned religious ceremonies.", "Rice is associated with Inari, the deity of agriculture and prosperity, and its cultivation and sharing are bound up with communal and divine significance.", "Rice is used exclusively in funeral ceremonies within the Shinto tradition, symbolising the transition from the physical world to the spiritual realm."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what distinguishes premium versions of the birth weight rice gift from standard versions?", options: ["Premium versions are designed to be planted rather than consumed, allowing the family to grow their own rice from the gifted seeds.", "Premium versions are produced exclusively by government-certified manufacturers and come with an official seal of cultural authenticity.", "Premium versions include a handwritten letter from the producer explaining the cultural history of the rice bag tradition in detail.", "Premium versions may incorporate prized rice varieties such as Koshihikari from Niigata prefecture, adding cultural prestige to the gift."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what demographic concern provides the broader context for the author's discussion of the rice bag tradition?", options: ["The increasing rate of emigration among young Japanese adults, which is reducing the domestic workforce and tax base significantly", "Japan's rapidly ageing population and the resulting pressure on public pension and healthcare systems for elderly citizens", "Japan's total fertility rate having fallen well below the replacement level of 2.1 children per woman, prompting government intervention", "The declining rural population in Japan's agricultural regions and its consequences for the continuation of rice cultivation traditions"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"benediction\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["a financial contribution made by a community to support a family during a significant life event such as a birth or wedding", "a traditional ceremony performed by a religious authority to mark the transition between one phase of life and another", "a formal legal document acknowledging the rights and obligations of individuals within a community or religious institution", "a spoken or symbolic expression of divine favour, blessing, or goodwill directed toward a person or occasion"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"instantiates\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["symbolically represents an abstract idea through a conventional image that has been agreed upon by members of a culture", "gradually transforms the meaning of an object over time as it is passed between different members of a community", "temporarily substitutes for something that is unavailable, serving as a placeholder until the original can be accessed directly", "makes something real and concretely present in physical form, giving tangible existence to something that would otherwise be absent"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a feature of commercially available birth weight rice gift products?", options: ["Online shipping services that allow family members living far away to send the gift within days of the birth", "A personalised video message from the producer addressed to the newborn child and their parents", "A certificate of authenticity confirming the weight of the rice enclosed in the bag", "Packaging printed with the child's name, date of birth, and birth weight"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'For recipients who have not yet met the child in person, this sensory experience of weight and warmth can serve as a powerful surrogate for the physical act of holding a newborn.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-135", num: 135, title: "Đề 135", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "The Silent Language of Body Movement", paragraphs: ["Long before people learn to speak, they already communicate through movement. A baby who reaches out its arms is asking to be held, and a toddler who turns away is showing that it does not want something. This kind of communication, known as body language, continues to play a huge role even after we learn to talk. In fact, many researchers believe that a large part of what we understand in daily conversation comes not from the actual words spoken, but from facial expressions, posture, and gestures.", "One of the clearest examples of body language is eye contact. In many Western countries, looking someone in the eye while speaking is seen as a sign of confidence and honesty. This is why job interviewers often pay close attention to whether a candidate maintains steady eye contact during a conversation. However, in several Asian and Middle Eastern cultures, prolonged eye contact, especially with someone older or in a position of authority, can be seen as rude or even challenging. A young employee who stares directly at a manager while being corrected might unintentionally appear disrespectful, even though no words of disrespect were used.", "Hand gestures also carry strong cultural meaning. A simple thumbs-up, which means approval in the United States, can be considered an offensive gesture in parts of the Middle East. Similarly, nodding the head usually means \"yes\" in most countries, but in Bulgaria and a few neighboring regions, the same nod can mean \"no.\" Travelers who are unaware of these differences sometimes find themselves confused during simple everyday exchanges, such as ordering food or asking for directions, because the gesture they rely on does not carry the same meaning locally.", "Posture is another powerful, though often overlooked , signal. Standing up straight with shoulders back is generally read as a sign of confidence, while slouching can suggest tiredness, boredom, or low self-esteem. Teachers and public speakers are frequently trained to stand tall and keep their arms relaxed, since a closed posture, such as crossed arms, can make an audience feel that the speaker is defensive or unwilling to connect with them. Interestingly, this signal appears to be understood similarly across many different cultures, suggesting that some elements of body language may be closer to universal than others.", "Facial expressions sit somewhere between these two extremes. Basic emotions such as happiness, anger, sadness, and surprise tend to produce remarkably similar facial expressions around the world, a finding that supports the idea that certain emotional signals are biologically built into us rather than learned. At the same time, how openly people are expected to show these expressions in public differs sharply from one society to another. In many East Asian cultures, for example, people are encouraged to keep their facial expressions calm and controlled, particularly in professional settings, while in many Mediterranean cultures, expressive faces and animated gestures are viewed as a normal and even desirable part of conversation.", "Given how much meaning is carried through body language, misunderstandings between people from different backgrounds are almost unavoidable at times. A gesture that feels completely natural in one place can easily be misread in another, leading to awkward or even embarrassing situations. For this reason, many international businesses now include short training sessions on body language as part of preparing employees for overseas assignments, helping them recognize that a polite smile, a firm handshake, or a respectful bow may carry very different weight depending on where they are."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Companies should train employees before sending them abroad", "Eye contact rules differ between Western and Asian countries", "Body language carries different meanings across cultures and can cause misunderstandings", "Facial expressions for basic emotions are the same everywhere"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do job interviewers in Western countries pay attention to eye contact?", options: ["Because it shows the candidate is nervous", "Because it helps interviewers remember the candidate's face", "Because steady eye contact is associated with confidence and honesty", "Because it is required by law in most companies"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What does the example of the thumbs-up gesture in paragraph 3 illustrate?", options: ["That travelers should avoid using hand gestures completely", "That hand gestures always mean the same thing worldwide", "That a gesture seen as positive in one country can be offensive elsewhere", "That nodding always means agreement everywhere"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What does crossed arms typically suggest to an audience, According to the passage?", options: ["That the speaker is defensive or reluctant to connect with listeners", "That the speaker is following standard public speaking rules", "That the speaker is confident and prepared", "That the speaker feels relaxed and friendly"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what supports the idea that some facial expressions may be biologically built in?", options: ["Basic emotions produce similar facial expressions across many cultures", "Mediterranean cultures value animated facial expressions", "People in East Asia are trained to control their expressions", "Professional settings require calm facial expressions"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following regions is NOT mentioned in connection with body language customs?", options: ["East Asia", "The Middle East", "South America", "Bulgaria"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"prolonged\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["extended", "polite", "nervous", "accidental"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"overlooked\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["trained", "measured", "criticized", "ignored"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"This\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["maintaining eye contact", "the hiring manager", "the job interview", "the candidate's confidence"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred from paragraph 6 about international businesses?", options: ["They require all employees to learn a single universal gesture system", "They believe body language training is unnecessary for most employees", "They prefer employees who avoid using gestures entirely", "They recognize that cultural misunderstandings about gestures can affect overseas work"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "Designing Tuition Structures Across Age Groups", paragraphs: ["When private learning centers and international schools set their pricing, one of the most consistent patterns observed across the education sector is that tuition fees are rarely uniform across all age groups. Instead, institutions typically design a tiered fee structure, with charges increasing as students progress from early childhood programs through to upper secondary or pre-university levels. This pattern may initially seem to suggest that older students simply require more expensive resources, but the underlying reasoning is considerably more complex than that.", "For preschool and kindergarten programs, fees tend to be comparatively lower, despite the fact that staffing ratios at this level are often the most demanding, with some centers maintaining one teacher for every six or seven children. The relatively modest pricing at this stage is largely a strategic decision: institutions aim to attract families early, building trust and a long-term relationship that may keep the child enrolled through subsequent, more profitable levels. This approach mirrors a common business strategy in which an initial offering is priced attractively to secure customer loyalty before pricing for related services increases over time.", "As students move into primary education, fees usually rise moderately, reflecting an expanded curriculum that now includes subjects such as foundational mathematics, language arts, and introductory science. Class sizes at this stage are typically larger than in early childhood programs, which partially offsets the additional cost of specialized teachers and materials, keeping fee increases relatively gradual rather than steep.", "The most noticeable jump in tuition often occurs at the secondary level, particularly for students preparing for standardized examinations or international qualifications such as IGCSE or IB programs. At this stage, institutions must invest heavily in subject specialists, laboratory equipment, examination administration, and university counseling services. Furthermore, smaller class sizes are frequently reintroduced at this level to allow for more individualized academic support, a factor that further drives up per-student costs. Parents are often willing to accept these higher charges because the perceived stakes, namely university admission and future career prospects, are considerably higher at this stage.", "Interestingly, some institutions adopt a markedly different model, charging a flat fee regardless of age, on the grounds that doing so simplifies administration and avoids the perception of unfairness among siblings of different ages within the same family. Critics of this flat-fee model argue, however, that it tends to disadvantage families with only younger children, who effectively subsidize the higher resource costs associated with older students without receiving equivalent benefit themselves.", "Financial aid policies further complicate the overall fee landscape. Many institutions reserve the bulk of their scholarship funding for older students, reasoning that retaining high-achieving teenagers, who are more visible in examination results and university placement statistics, brings greater reputational benefit to the school than retaining younger children would. As a result, families with younger children sometimes find that financial assistance is comparatively scarce, even when household income would otherwise qualify them for support at other institutions.", "Ultimately, tuition structures across age groups reveal as much about an institution's long-term business strategy and reputational priorities as they do about the direct cost of delivering education at each level. Families navigating these systems benefit from looking beyond the listed price for each age group and instead considering the broader incentives that shape why fees are arranged the way they are."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main purpose of the passage?", options: ["To explain the strategic and business reasons behind tiered tuition pricing across age groups", "To argue that flat-fee tuition models are always superior", "To criticize schools for charging unfair tuition fees", "To compare the cost of preschool programs in different countries"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why are preschool fees often kept relatively low despite demanding staffing ratios?", options: ["Preschool teachers require less training than other staff", "Preschool programs receive more public funding than other levels", "Institutions want to attract families early and build long-term loyalty", "Government regulations require lower preschool pricing"], answer: 2 }, { q: "Why do fees tend to rise sharply at the secondary level, According to the passage?", options: ["Because class sizes become significantly larger", "Because secondary students require fewer resources than younger students", "Because institutions invest in specialists, equipment, and smaller classes for exam preparation", "Because parents demand higher fees to ensure better treatment"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is one criticism of the flat-fee tuition model?", options: ["It causes conflict between siblings of the same family", "It can disadvantage families with only younger children", "It makes school administration more complicated", "It increases overall tuition costs for everyone"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Why do many institutions reserve more scholarship funding for older students, according to the passage?", options: ["Government policy requires schools to prioritize older students", "Younger students rarely qualify for financial assistance", "Older students are statistically more likely to need financial aid", "High-achieving older students bring greater reputational benefit through exam and university results"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a factor driving up secondary-level tuition?", options: ["Subject specialist teachers", "Boarding accommodation costs", "Smaller class sizes", "University counseling services"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"modest\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["temporary", "competitive", "moderate", "unstable"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"offsets\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["removes entirely", "increases sharply", "balances out", "delays"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"This\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["the number of enrolled children", "pricing early programs low to build loyalty", "the staffing ratio at preschools", "the cost of teacher training"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward tuition pricing structures?", options: ["Strongly critical of all institutions that use tiered pricing", "Fully supportive of flat-fee pricing as the fairest model", "Analytical, encouraging families to look beyond listed prices to understand underlying incentives", "Indifferent, since pricing has little real impact on families"], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "English as a Global Lingua Franca", paragraphs: ["English occupies a peculiar position among the world's languages. [1] Unlike most other widely spoken languages, its global reach was not driven primarily by the number of people who speak it as a mother tongue. Instead, English has become what linguists term a lingua franca, a shared medium that allows speakers of entirely different native languages to communicate, often in contexts where neither party grew up speaking it at all. A Japanese engineer negotiating a contract with a Brazilian supplier, for instance, is far more likely to conduct that exchange in English than in either Japanese or Portuguese, even though English is foreign to both parties.", "The historical roots of this phenomenon trace back to the colonial expansion of the British Empire, which established English-language administration, education, and commerce across vast territories in Asia, Africa, and the Americas. [2] However, colonial history alone does not fully explain English's continued dominance, since many former colonies have since developed robust local-language education systems. A second, arguably more decisive factor was the economic and technological ascendancy of the United States throughout the twentieth century, which embedded English within international finance, scientific publishing, aviation, and, later, computing and the internet.", "One consequence of this spread is that the number of people who use English as a second or additional language now substantially exceeds the number of native speakers. This has prompted scholars to distinguish between what is sometimes called \"native-speaker English,\" rooted in the cultural and idiomatic norms of countries such as the United Kingdom or the United States, and \"International English,\" a more streamlined variety used primarily for cross-border communication, stripped of much of the idiomatic complexity that can confuse non-native interlocutors. Researchers studying this shift argue that ownership of the language has, in effect, been redistributed away from its traditional native-speaking populations.", "This redistribution carries pedagogical implications that are still being debated. [3] Some applied linguists contend that English instruction worldwide should shift its benchmark away from native-speaker fluency, on the grounds that the overwhelming majority of English interactions globally occur between non-native speakers who do not require, and arguably do not benefit from, mastery of idioms or accents specific to any single English-speaking country. Opponents of this view counter that abandoning a clear standard risks fragmenting the language into mutually unintelligible regional varieties, thereby undermining the very communicative function that gave English its global utility in the first place.", "The economic stakes attached to English proficiency are considerable and unevenly distributed. In many countries, fluency in English correlates strongly with access to higher-paying employment, particularly in multinational corporations, technology firms, and academia, where English frequently serves as the working language even when no native English speakers are present in the room. [4] This has given rise to a substantial private tutoring industry in regions such as East Asia and parts of the Middle East, where families invest heavily in English education, sometimes from early childhood, in the hope of securing future advantages for their children. Critics of this trend caution that it can deepen existing inequalities, since families with greater financial resources are able to purchase superior instruction, thereby converting economic privilege into linguistic advantage and, in turn, further economic opportunity.", "Despite its dominance, English's global status is not universally regarded as benign . Some sociolinguists argue that the language's spread has contributed to the erosion of linguistic diversity, as smaller languages lose prestige and, in some cases, speakers, when English is positioned as the language of opportunity and modernity. Others push back against this characterization, noting that multilingualism remains the global norm rather than the exception, and that English typically functions alongside local languages rather than replacing them outright, occupying a specific domain, such as international business or higher education, while local languages continue to dominate domestic and everyday life.", "What seems clear is that English's trajectory over the coming decades will be shaped less by any single government's language policy than by the cumulative, decentralized choices of millions of individuals and institutions deciding, often for pragmatic rather than ideological reasons, which language best serves their immediate communicative needs. Whether this gradual, bottom-up process ultimately entrenches English's position further or eventually gives way to a more genuinely multipolar linguistic landscape, in which no single language dominates international communication, remains an open and contested question among scholars of global language policy."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Private tutoring is the main reason English has spread globally", "Native speakers should set the standard for all English instruction worldwide", "English's global spread as a lingua franca stems from multiple historical and economic forces, and its future remains debated", "English became dominant solely because of British colonialism"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What best explains why English continued to dominate even after colonial rule ended?", options: ["The United States' economic and technological rise embedded English in global institutions beyond former colonies", "Former colonies abandoned their local languages entirely", "The British government continued funding English education abroad", "International organizations mandated English as an official language"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what distinguishes \"International English\" from \"native-speaker English\"?", options: ["International English replaces native-speaker English in academic publishing entirely", "International English is only used in former British colonies", "International English is grammatically simpler in every respect", "International English is reduced in idiomatic complexity to aid cross-border communication among non-native speakers"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What concern do opponents raise regarding the proposal to move away from native-speaker benchmarks?", options: ["It would prevent multinational companies from using English at all", "It could lead to regional varieties of English becoming mutually unintelligible", "It would make English too difficult for non-native learners", "It would reduce funding for English-language education programs"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does English proficiency relate to economic inequality?", options: ["Governments equally fund English education for all income levels", "It has no measurable connection to employment or income", "English fluency is now required by law for all professional jobs", "Wealthier families can purchase better English instruction, converting financial advantage into further economic opportunity"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a domain in which English serves as a working language?", options: ["National judicial systems", "International finance", "Scientific publishing", "Aviation"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"ascendancy\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["formal agreement", "rise to dominance", "temporary setback", "gradual decline"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"benign\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["profitable", "inevitable", "widespread", "harmless"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What is the author's overall purpose in the passage?", options: ["To prove that linguistic diversity is no longer at risk anywhere", "To argue that English should be replaced by a neutral artificial language", "To convince readers to abandon learning their native languages", "To present the complex historical, economic, and social dimensions of English's global spread without taking a definitive side"], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Yet this advantage is not distributed equally within societies, raising questions of fairness that extend well beyond language itself.'", options: ["[4]", "[2]", "[3]", "[1]"], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "Rethinking the Urban Bus as Infrastructure", paragraphs: ["For decades, transportation planners and the general public alike have regarded the bus as the unglamorous workhorse of urban mobility, a stopgap solution overshadowed by the perceived prestige of rail systems. Yet a growing body of scholarship in urban planning argues that this perception fundamentally misrepresents the bus's potential, not as a vehicle, but as a flexible platform upon which an entire category of high-performing transit infrastructure can be built. The deterioration of this reputation, researchers suggest, stems less from any inherent limitation of bus technology than from decades of underinvestment, poor route design, and political reluctance to allocate dedicated road space to a mode of transport widely perceived as serving primarily lower-income populations.", "Central to this reassessment is the concept of Bus Rapid Transit, commonly abbreviated as BRT, a system that seeks to replicate the operational reliability of rail through infrastructural rather than technological means. By segregating buses into dedicated lanes, implementing off-board fare collection, and elevating platforms to allow level boarding, BRT systems can achieve passenger throughput and journey-time consistency that rivals light rail, often at a fraction of the capital cost. Cities such as Bogotá and Curitiba are frequently cited in the planning literature not merely as successful implementations but as proof of concept that the constraint limiting bus performance was never the vehicle itself, but rather the absence of infrastructure that grants it priority over private automobile traffic.", "Not only does dedicated infrastructure improve service reliability, but it also alters the broader political economy of transit investment. Once a municipality commits substantial road space exclusively to bus operations, the resulting visible, permanent infrastructure tends to generate a constituency of riders and adjacent property owners who advocate for the system's continuation and expansion, a dynamic that arguably does not necessarily imply equivalent long-term political durability for less visible service improvements, such as simple frequency increases on existing mixed-traffic routes. This asymmetry helps explain why some transit agencies pursue costly infrastructural overhauls even when more modest operational changes might, in narrowly economic terms, deliver comparable ridership gains at lower cost.", "The relationship between bus systems and land use further complicates any straightforward cost-benefit assessment. Whereas rail investment has long been associated with measurable increases in adjacent property values and transit-oriented development, the evidence for BRT's capacity to generate similar land-use transformation remains decidedly mixed, varying significantly according to the perceived permanence of the infrastructure. Where BRT corridors are built with substantial, rail-like physical infrastructure, including elevated stations and dedicated rights-of-way that would be costly to remove, developers appear more willing to invest nearby on the assumption that the service will persist over the long timescales relevant to real estate decisions. Where BRT is implemented through more easily reversible measures, such as painted lanes, this development response is considerably weaker, suggesting that it is not bus service per se that attracts investment but rather a credible signal of infrastructural permanence.", "A further dimension, frequently underexamined in popular discourse, concerns the social and symbolic dimensions of bus travel relative to rail. Survey research across multiple countries has consistently found that travelers report a preference for rail over bus service even when objective measures, including speed, frequency, and reliability, are held constant, a phenomenon some scholars attribute to what might be termed an infrastructural legibility effect, wherein the fixed, visible nature of rail tracks communicates permanence and institutional commitment in a manner that buses, which can be rerouted or withdrawn with comparatively little cost, do not. This perception gap arguably does not merely reflect irrational bias but may capture a genuine, if difficult to quantify, risk premium that riders implicitly assign to a mode of transport whose continuation is less institutionally guaranteed.", "These findings carry significant implications for how cities allocate scarce transportation budgets. Proponents of bus-based systems caution against interpreting persistent rider preference for rail as evidence that rail is intrinsically superior, arguing instead that such preferences are themselves partly a product of historical underinvestment in bus infrastructure, and that closing the perception gap may require not merely improving bus service quality but deliberately constructing visible, costly-to-reverse infrastructure that signals comparable institutional commitment. Critics of this position counter that diverting limited capital toward infrastructurally heavy bus systems risks reproducing many of the cost overruns associated with rail while sacrificing the operational flexibility, namely the capacity to adjust routes in response to shifting demand, that constitutes one of the bus mode's most genuine comparative advantages.", "Ultimately, the debate over the urban bus's proper role extrapolates beyond questions of engineering or cost efficiency alone, touching on deeper questions about how cities signal long-term commitment to particular populations and corridors through the durability of the infrastructure they choose to build. Whether bus-based systems can ever fully substantiate the institutional credibility long associated with rail, or whether some residual perception gap is destined to persist regardless of investment, remains an unresolved empirical question, one that current research has not yet definitively settled despite several decades of comparative transit studies across diverse urban contexts."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main argument of the passage?", options: ["BRT systems are identical in every respect to traditional rail lines", "Rail transit is always more cost-effective than bus systems", "Cities should abandon bus systems in favor of rail wherever possible", "The bus's poor reputation stems mainly from underinvestment, and dedicated infrastructure can transform its performance and perception"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what allows BRT systems to rival light rail in performance?", options: ["Infrastructural changes such as dedicated lanes and off-board fare collection rather than new vehicle technology", "Replacing bus drivers with automated systems", "Reducing the number of stops along each route", "Using larger buses with greater passenger capacity"], answer: 0 }, { q: "Why might a transit agency choose costly infrastructural overhauls over cheaper operational improvements?", options: ["Operational improvements are illegal in most cities", "Costly overhauls always produce higher ridership in the short term", "Visible, permanent infrastructure builds a lasting political constituency and signals credibility that cheaper measures do not", "Operational improvements require more government approval than infrastructure projects"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do painted-lane BRT corridors generate weaker land-use development than corridors with elevated stations?", options: ["Painted lanes are only used in cities with declining populations", "Painted lanes are more expensive to construct", "Elevated stations reduce traffic congestion more effectively", "Developers see painted lanes as a less permanent, more easily reversible commitment"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What does the \"infrastructural legibility effect\" suggest about rider preferences?", options: ["Riders prefer rail purely because it is faster than buses in all cases", "The fixed, visible nature of rail signals institutional permanence, influencing preference independent of objective performance", "Preference for rail is solely caused by ticket pricing differences", "Riders are unable to distinguish between bus and rail service quality"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a feature of Bus Rapid Transit systems?", options: ["Dedicated bus lanes", "Automated driverless vehicles", "Elevated boarding platforms", "Off-board fare collection"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"deterioration\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["decline", "expansion", "renovation", "regulation"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"extrapolates\" in paragraph 7 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["resolves completely", "summarizes briefly", "extends beyond", "contradicts entirely"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What is the author's attitude toward the debate over bus versus rail infrastructure investment?", options: ["Balanced and analytical, presenting competing arguments without declaring a definitive resolution", "Indifferent, suggesting the topic has no real policy significance", "Dismissive, treating the debate as settled in favor of rail", "Strongly advocating for eliminating all rail investment"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"that\" in paragraph 6 refers to _______.", options: ["capital budget allocation", "closing the perception gap", "rider preference for rail", "the BRT corridor design"], answer: 1 }] },
  ] },
];
